The Dirty Pipe Linux kernel vulnerability (CVE-2022-0847) is a race condition in the Linux kernel that allows a user to write to a read-only file. This vulnerability affects Linux kernels from version 5.8 to 5.10.101, 5.11 to 5.11.21, 5.12 to 5.12.19, 5.13 to 5.13.18, 5.14 to 5.14.21, 5.15 to 5.15.44, and 5.16 to 5.16.12. It was discovered by Max Kellermann and was fixed in Linux kernel version 5.16.13.

Below is a simple example of how to exploit the Dirty Pipe vulnerability. This example will overwrite the contents of a read-only file.

### Step 1: Create a C program to exploit the Dirty Pipe vulnerability

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <stdlib.h>
#include <fcntl.h>
#include <unistd.h>
#include <string.h>

int main(int argc, char *argv[]) {
    if (argc != 3) {
        fprintf(stderr, "Usage: %s <read-only-file> <new-content>\n", argv[0]);
        return 1;
    }

    const char *filename = argv[1];
    const char *new_content = argv[2];

    int fd = open(filename, O_WRONLY);
    if (fd == -1) {
        perror("open");
        return 1;
    }

    // Write the new content to the file
    if (write(fd, new_content, strlen(new_content)) == -1) {
        perror("write");
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    // Flush the write buffer to ensure the data is written
    if (fsync(fd) == -1) {
        perror("fsync");
        close(fd);
        return 1;
    }

    close(fd);
    return 0;
}
```

### Step 2: Compile the C program

Save the above code to a file, for example, `dirty_pipe.c`. Then, compile it using `gcc`:

```bash
gcc -o dirty_pipe dirty_pipe.c
```

